// Formative practice quiz for commits-diffs-and-gitignore. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/commits-diffs-and-gitignore

::commits-diffs-and-gitignore o1 - where a staged edit lives::You edited README.md, saved it and ran git add README.md. You have not committed yet. Where does your edit exist right now?{
=In the working copy and in the staging area, but in no commit yet#Correct. git add copies the file into the staging area and leaves your working copy as it is. Only git commit puts the staged snapshot into history.
~Only in the staging area, because git add moved it out of the working copy#Not quite. git add copies the file; it does not move it. The edited file is still on disk in your working copy.
~In a new commit, because git add saves the change to history#Not quite. git add only prepares the next commit. Nothing reaches history until you run git commit.
~Only in the working copy, because git add just marks the file for later#Not quite. git add copies the file content at that moment into the staging area, so the staging area holds a real snapshot of it.
}

::commits-diffs-and-gitignore o2 - preview the next commit::You staged login.js, then kept editing login.js and saved again. Before committing, you want to see exactly what the next commit will contain. Which command shows that?{
=git diff --staged#Correct. git diff --staged compares the staging area with the last commit, which is exactly what the next commit will hold.
~git diff#Not quite. git diff compares the working copy with the staging area, so it shows only your newer, unstaged edit.
~git status#Not quite. git status lists which files differ in each place, but it does not show the changed lines.
~git diff, because it shows staged and unstaged changes together#Not quite. Plain git diff leaves out what is already staged; it shows only changes not yet added.
}

::commits-diffs-and-gitignore o2 - make a focused commit::You changed cart.js (the bug fix), notes.txt (personal notes) and style.css (an unfinished experiment). You want a commit that holds only the cart.js fix. What do you run?{
=git add cart.js, then git commit -m "Fix cart"#Correct. Staging the file by name puts only cart.js in the staging area, and the commit records only what is staged. The other edits stay in the working copy.
~git add ., then git diff --staged, then git commit -m "Fix cart"#Not quite. git add . stages every changed file that is not ignored. Checking with git diff --staged shows the problem but does not unstage anything, so all three files are committed.
~git commit -m "Fix cart" without git add, because Git commits the most recent change#Not quite. A commit records only the staging area. With nothing staged, Git reports that no changes were added to commit.
~git add ., then git commit -m "Fix cart", because Git skips files you did not mean to change#Not quite. Git cannot know your intent. git add . stages everything not ignored, so notes.txt and style.css would be committed too.
}

::commits-diffs-and-gitignore o3 - ignore a file Git already tracks::Last week someone committed .env by mistake. Today you add the line .env to .gitignore and commit .gitignore. You then edit .env. What does git status show?{
=.env as modified, because .gitignore does not untrack a file Git already has#Correct. Ignore rules apply only to untracked files. A file that is already tracked stays tracked, and it also stays in the commits that hold it.
~Nothing about .env, because it now matches a .gitignore pattern#Not quite. That is what happens to an untracked file. .env is already tracked, so Git keeps reporting its changes.
~.env as deleted, because committing .gitignore removes matching files from the repository#Not quite. .gitignore never removes or untracks anything; it only keeps untracked files out of git status and git add .
~An error, because a tracked file cannot appear in .gitignore#Not quite. Git accepts the pattern without complaint. It simply has no effect on a file Git already tracks.
}
